<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti</h1><p class="st-meta">1762 CE – 1801 CE · capital Tbilisi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last independent Georgian kingdom in the east, created by Erekle II, which placed itself under Russian protection in 1783 and was annexed in 1801.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kingdom of Kakheti</a> since 1744, succeeded his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teimuraz-ii-of-kakheti">Teimuraz II</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kartli">Kingdom of Kartli</a> in 1762, uniting eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> under one crown for the first time since the 15th century. He had gained prestige fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and against Dagestani raiders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle II</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, modernised the army, encouraged trade and crafts and drew tribute from the khanates of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja</a> and Erivan. In 1783 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-georgievsk">Treaty of Georgievsk</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, accepting a Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> failed to defend him, and in 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Muhammad Khan Qajar</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-krtsanisi">Krtsanisi</a> and sacked Tbilisi.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erekle-ii">Erekle</a>'s death in 1798, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-xii-of-georgia">George XII</a> asked for closer union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. When George died in 1800, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-i-of-russia">Emperor Paul I</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> annexed the kingdom in 1801, deposing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagratid</a> dynasty and making it a Russian province.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kartli-kakheti.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Althiphika · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kingdom_of_Kartli-Kakheti.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1762 CE – 1801 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tbilisi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Erekle II, George XII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kartli-Kakheti, Georgian Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1782&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kartli-kakheti" aria-label="Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5771 691 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kartli-Kakheti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6091 947L6110 938M6091 947L6091 947M6091 947L6487 457M6091 947L6080 935M6091 947L6136 981M6091 947L6095 997"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6110 938h0M6091 947h0M6487 457h0M6080 935h0M6136 981h0M6095 997h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6091 947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6091 947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot